{
	title: 'Thinking effort per model',
	spec: `A model selection is a canonical provider/model id plus an optional explicit effort. Every place accepting a model uses the same resolver and validation: /model, spawn model override, config model default and aliases. Accept alias:level and provider/model:level, e.g. opus:low, gpt:high and openai/gpt-6.1-sol:xhigh; :default clears the override and uses the provider policy, not a wire effort value. Alias expansion happens on the base model before effort validation. The generic gpt alias resolves to its deliberately promoted current target (GPT-6.1 Sol now) before validating effort; pinned version aliases retain their own targets. Literal model ids containing colons (notably Ollama tags such as ollama/qwen3:8b and OpenRouter :free) keep working: exact known ids win; otherwise only the final recognized effort suffix is parsed and the remaining full base id stays intact. An unknown suffix attached to a known base/alias is an actionable error, not a silent effort downgrade. Explicit unsupported levels fail before changing metadata or sending a request and list the allowed levels.

One overridable capability function keyed by provider AND bare model owns supported ordered levels, default request policy and wire mapping. Reuse provider/catalog metadata when it actually declares effort values; a generic reasoning=true flag is not proof that any particular effort is supported. Unknown models/servers keep current behavior with no extra field; they offer no effort selection until an endpoint hook or verified metadata supplies it. The ordered vocabulary is none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max, filtered per model; adaptive is a thinking mode, not an effort. Do not equate none with low, or max with xhigh except when the provider documents that mapping. Defaults for unqualified selections preserve current Hal request policy unless invalid for that model; display the actual configured policy rather than pretending that an omitted field is an explicitly requested level.

Frontier mappings are explicit: OpenAI Responses uses reasoning.effort, retaining summary and encrypted reasoning replay. GPT-6.1 Sol and GPT-6 Astra allow low/medium/high/xhigh/max; GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna also allow none; none of these accepts minimal. GPT-5, 5.1–5.6, Codex and o-series use their own documented/catalog-supported sets, not a /^gpt/ universal set. The ChatGPT backend may expose narrower model/effort availability than the API, so intersect authoritative backend capability data when available without assuming API-key availability implies subscription availability. Anthropic Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 use adaptive thinking plus output_config.effort low/medium/high/xhigh/max, never manual budget_tokens or thinking disabled. API defaults are medium for Opus 5.5 and high for Sonnet 5.5, distinguished from any Hal-selected defaults. Adaptive-only Opus 4.7+, Opus 5, Sonnet 5 and Fable families retain their own supported sets; 4.6 has no xhigh. Older manual-thinking models map supported UI levels to explicit documented token budgets through the capability hook (minimum 1024, strictly below max_tokens), and none disables thinking only where legal. No false precision in historic headers: effort records the selected level, not a retrospectively guessed label for old thinking.

OpenAI-compatible endpoints are not interchangeable. OpenRouter uses reasoning.effort and its GET /models reasoning.supported_efforts/default_effort/mandatory metadata, honoring omission versus null and hiding none when mandatory. OpenCode Go uses a verified per-model mapping (e.g. current GLM-5.2 high/max via reasoning_effort); do not advertise invented levels for Kimi/GLM/DeepSeek/Qwen merely because another provider supports them. Ollama uses model-defined reasoning_effort on /v1/chat/completions: gpt-oss low/medium/high; boolean-thinking models expose none/high only after documented conversion/verification. Preserve Ollama model tags. Custom endpoints can supply capability and mapping functions via local.ts/plugins without dependencies. A selected setting must actually reach the body; unsupported fields are never silently dropped while UI claims they were used.

Session metadata persists the model and effort override atomically through liveFiles; snapshots and client protocol carry the effective selection. Resuming/restarting keeps it without changing defaults for other sessions. Changing to an unqualified model resets to that models default, never leaks an old incompatible effort. A fresh spawn with no model override inherits the parents current model+effort; a spawn with its own unqualified model uses that models default, and a qualified override uses its specified effort. Forks inherit the pair. Config supports qualified model strings through the same parser. A running provider request freezes model+effort at its request boundary; changes take effect on the next request, not on already-streaming header facts. Task hp live/history model+effort fields capture the request actually sent; transcript replay never changes old effort. Terminal and web status rows show the current effective effort next to the model (or default/unknown where the provider sets no known effort), distinct from a still-running block.

Effort changes append the existing model-change metadata note without rewriting old history or the system prompt. This task uses ordinary request-level effort changes, with a concise notice when Anthropic changing top-level effort invalidates its provider-rendered cache prefix; cache-preserving beta per-message configuration is deliberately separate future work, not guessed across subscription/gateway transports. No reasoning.mode/pro control, between_tools toggle, automatic per-turn effort routing, numeric budget form or new cycle shortcut in this task. Tests focus on qualifier ambiguity, rejected provider/model combinations, mapped request bodies, persistence/spawn and immutable streaming/replay facts, using fake transports rather than paid model output.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Current API sources checked 2026-09-30: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning and https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol , /gpt-6-astra , /gpt-6-sol , /gpt-6-luna . All allow max (the older six-level enum in the request is incomplete); none/minimal are forbidden for 6.1 Sol/Astra, Sol/Luna permit none but not minimal. Reasoning mode pro is independent, not another effort. Responses configuration_update preserves cached prefixes in GPT-6 standard single-agent mode, must retain original top-level effort and historical update positions, forbids adjacent updates and automatic truncation/compaction; standalone compact endpoint rejects histories containing updates. This is intentionally not part of baseline w7.',
		'Anthropic sources: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort ; https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/opus-5-5/whats-new-opus-5-5 ; https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/sonnet-5-5/whats-new-sonnet-5-5 . Both 5.5s support low/medium/high/xhigh/max via output_config.effort; reject manual budget_tokens. Opus 5.5 always thinks, default medium. Sonnet 5.5 API default high; between_tools replaces disabled at low..high, rejects xhigh/max and per-message changing effort. Both offer beta per-message effort via mid-conversation-output-config-2026-07-01 and empty-content system message output_config, preserving cache; request-level changes restart it. New thinking blocks can be prefix-bound: do not rewrite older messages while stripping display metadata. Adaptive display defaults omitted on these models; summarized makes reasoning/progress text visible. Preserve signatures regardless of visibility.',
		'Harness UX: Claude Code /effort and --effort, /model picker left/right, effortLevel setting and CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL; current 5.5 coding defaults medium, distinct from Sonnet API high. Source https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config . Pi /thinking, Shift+Tab cycle, --thinking, --model sonnet:high, per-model settings and session thinking_level_change records; levels off/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh/max filtered by capabilities. Sources https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/1b347794e2a630e4359f2584f4eea388145d0ddf/packages/coding-agent/docs/models.md , docs/cli.md , docs/settings.md , docs/keybindings.md . Codex /model opens model then supported effort child picker, config model_reasoning_effort, catalog-supported levels and current/default labels. Source https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2a34aef79484bf769b4b225f3f595755f2962170/codex-rs/tui/src/chatwidget/model_popups.rs .',
		'Compatible-provider sources: https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/best-practices/reasoning-tokens (per-model reasoning.supported_efforts: omitted means no effort control, null means all gateway values; mandatory excludes none; max/xhigh may share budget allocation). https://docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility (reasoning_effort model-defined aliases, preserve model tags). https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/f66b86ceec1a497417f750b88a06cf6923c5c75f/packages/opencode/src/provider/transform.ts (current OpenAI-compatible GLM-5.2 high/max; many earlier Kimi/Qwen/Minimax/GLM transports return no variants). Current Hal src/host/openai-compat.ts defaults are OpenRouter, OpenCode Go and Ollama; none currently sends effort. OpenCode Go non-GLM-5.2 controls remain provider/model-specific and must be verified rather than guessed from issue reports.',
		'Old Hal reference artifacts: old-models.ts reasoningEffort hardcodes xhigh for codex, high for /^o/ or /^gpt-[56]/; old-openai.ts buildResponsesBody actually sends it. old-anthropic.ts uses adaptive on newer families, otherwise fixed 10000-token budget, without output_config.effort. old-history.ts stores thinkingEffort on thinking records; runtime captures models.reasoningEffort at the start of a model response and attaches it to live and history events. These are reference, not code to lift: broad family guesses are precisely what w7 replaces, and hp already owns immutable header facts.',
		'Implemented capability/wire policy in src/host/effort.ts with browser-safe descriptions in common/effort.ts. Provider/Endpoint capability hooks override verified tables, including narrower backend sets when available; current ChatGPT catalog exposes no authoritative effort sets, so no subscription availability is invented. Unknown models retain existing unqualified behavior and reject explicit efforts. OpenRouter effort metadata is kept in memory with its model listing; omitted/null and mandatory are distinct. Metadata stores bare model plus optional effort atomically; change notes use qualified strings, not new historic record types. Streaming events now carry request model/effort on each round so a within-turn switch cannot relabel earlier blocks.',
		'Real frontier probes were not executed: harness protected-credential guards refused both bash and eval access, even after coordinator approval. Coordinator requested stopping rather than bypassing the guard and will verify after a restart. Fake-transport/body tests cover both frontier providers; no credential contents or host configuration are recorded.',
		'Gateway restart correction: verified OpenRouter descriptors also persist through liveFiles in a small effort catalog. This supersedes the earlier in-memory-only note: a saved override must still validate and reach reasoning.effort immediately after restart, without waiting for HTTP discovery. A successful listing replaces the cached authoritative descriptors; absent supported_efforts removes control. The fake gateway test clears in-memory state and restores a mapped request from disk with HTTP disabled.',
		'Provider tables additionally checked against current docs: GPT-5.2/5.4 default none; GPT-5.5 default medium with none/low/medium/high/xhigh; GPT-5.6 Sol/Terra/Luna and alias default medium with none/low/medium/high/xhigh/max; 5.2/5.3 Codex exclude none. Anthropic effort docs confirm Sonnet 4.6 supports max but not xhigh, and Fable 5.1 supports all five. Older manual UI levels choose legal budgets 1024/4096/10000 and reject a budget at or above max_tokens; they are selected UI levels, not claims about historic token counts. Sources: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.2 , /gpt-5.4 , /gpt-5.5 , /gpt-5.6-sol , /gpt-5.6-luna , /gpt-5.2-codex , /gpt-5.3-codex and https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort .',
		"Live check after a restart (orchestrator 158-ash): opus-5-5:low, sonnet-5-5:high and gpt-6-sol:none each answered a one-line prompt through provider.stream; gpt-6.1-sol:low got HTTP 429 (the account's usage limit), so its effort mapping is untested live.",
		"Bug fix, invariant: an explicitly selected effort always reaches the wire, even when it equals the default Hal believes the model has. effort.nearest used to clear a level matching cap.default, so gpt-6.1-sol:medium sent no reasoning.effort and the Codex backend applied its own default, low (backend model metadata default_reasoning_level 'low', 8 Oct). Only :default clears the override."
	],
	needs: ['03', '7f', 'qq', 'hp', 'pc', 't0', '68', 'px']
